Say what the device tree pointer points at

The whole storage path depends on this one number, and nothing has ever
printed it.  XNU does not read boot_args.deviceTreeP itself: PE_init_platform
copies it into PE_state.deviceTreeHead and hands it to SecureDTInit(), and
every device tree lookup afterwards -- the /arm-io children, the UFS host and
PHY nodes, /chosen/dram-base -- reads whatever SecureDTInit was given.

The boot arguments are not in the kernel image: const_boot_args is all zeroes
in the file and is filled at run time from the structure the previous stage
hands over in x0.  On this board that previous stage is the stock Android
bootloader, because the kernel is booted with `fastboot boot`, and it has no
reason to fill in an iOS device tree.  So the tree the kernel sees is
whatever those bytes happen to be, which is why /chosen/dram-base reads a
different number on every boot and why the UFS controller has never been
matched: there is no node to match.

So print the pointer and the length.  Nothing more: this runs before
SecureDTInit, and at this point in the boot the address is not necessarily
mapped, so reading through it is a data abort.  A first version of this
printed the tree's contents by walking them, and the kernel died on the
first byte of it -- the boot with that version produced no output at all, not
one character on the panel, while the same kernel without it printed a full
screen.

---

diff --git a/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c b/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
--- a/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
+++ b/pexpert/arm/pe_init.c
@@ -400,6 +400,40 @@
 		PE_state.bootArgs = boot_args_ptr;
 		PE_state.deviceTreeHead = boot_args_ptr->deviceTreeP;
 		PE_state.deviceTreeSize = boot_args_ptr->deviceTreeLength;
+		/*
+		 * nio: say what the device tree pointer points at.
+		 *
+		 * The whole storage path depends on this one number, and nothing
+		 * has ever printed it.  XNU does not read boot_args.deviceTreeP
+		 * itself: PE_init_platform copies it into PE_state.deviceTreeHead
+		 * and hands it to SecureDTInit(), and every device tree lookup
+		 * afterwards -- the /arm-io children, the UFS host and PHY nodes,
+		 * /chosen/dram-base -- reads whatever SecureDTInit was given.
+		 *
+		 * The boot arguments are not in the kernel image: const_boot_args
+		 * is all zeroes in the file and is filled at run time from the
+		 * structure the previous stage hands over in x0.  On this board
+		 * that previous stage is the stock Android bootloader, because the
+		 * kernel is booted with `fastboot boot`, and it has no reason to
+		 * fill in an iOS device tree.  So the tree the kernel sees is
+		 * whatever those bytes happen to be, which is why /chosen/dram-base
+		 * reads a different number on every boot and why the UFS
+		 * controller has never been matched: there is no node to match.
+		 *
+		 * So print the pointer and the length.  Nothing more: this runs
+		 * before SecureDTInit, and at this point in the boot the address
+		 * is not necessarily mapped, so reading through it is a data
+		 * abort.  A first version of this printed the tree's contents by
+		 * walking them, and the kernel died on the first byte of it --
+		 * the boot with that version produced no output at all, not one
+		 * character on the panel, while the same kernel without it
+		 * printed a full screen.
+		 */
+		printf("\nnio: dt head=%p len=%u virtBase=0x%llx physBase=0x%llx",
+		    (void *)PE_state.deviceTreeHead, PE_state.deviceTreeSize,
+		    (unsigned long long)boot_args_ptr->virtBase,
+		    (unsigned long long)boot_args_ptr->physBase);
+		printf("\n");
 		PE_state.video.v_baseAddr = boot_args_ptr->Video.v_baseAddr;
 		PE_state.video.v_rowBytes = boot_args_ptr->Video.v_rowBytes;
 		PE_state.video.v_width = boot_args_ptr->Video.v_width;
